description:
FILED s537b (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-11) from member-1 / member-3 probes.  PRE-EXISTING on main 3d524ad7 (scratch/s537b/pr/m1j.pl m3i.pl m3j.pl m3k.pl in the s537b worktree).

REPRODUCERS (probed vs perl 5.40.3):

    use feature 'state';
    sub f { state %k = (k => 'v'); return "@{[ %k ]}|@{[ scalar(keys %k) ]}" }
    print f(), "\n";                      # perl: k v|1        main: |0   (SILENT)

    my %h = (a => 1);
    package Q;
    print "@{[ %h ]}|@{[ scalar(keys %h) ]}|@{[ $h{a} ]}\n";
                                          # perl: a 1|1|1     main: The variable %h is unbound (raw backtrace)

    my %h = (a => 1); { my %h; }
    package Q;
    print "@{[ %h ]}|@{[ scalar(keys %h) ]}\n";   # perl: a 1|1   main: refuses? no -- prints |0 (SILENT: no span seen)

TWO halves, both "a `%name` inside an interpolation CODE block is a use":
1. The BINDER (Pl::Binder, refs via `interp-code`) records `$h{a}` / `@l` / `$s` inside `@{[ ]}` / `${\ }`, but not a bare `%h` (PCL_BINDER_TRACE=1 on m3i.pl: no ref at line 3; on m3k.pl ONE %h ref for three mentions).  So the span pass never sees the later-section use (no promotion, no refusal) and the capture gate misses it.
2. The rename families' text rewrite `Parser2::_interp_fixer` has no `%name` arm (the `%` arm rewrites `$h{` and `@h{` only) -- the classic `state %k` renames the declaration and leaves `%k` in the code block on the global.

FIX DIRECTION: one reading -- Pl::InterpScan reports the code inside `@{[ ]}` / `${\ }` (it must already delimit it for `$h{a}`); the binder takes every variable token of that code (a re-parse of the block text, like `fragment_doc`), and the fixer rewrites `%name` only inside such a code region (never in plain string text: "100%k" is literal).  Standing rule §8: interpolation scanning happens in Pl/InterpScan.pm.
=== NOTE s540 (the reviewing session, 2026-11-13) === since s537b (MAIN-READY-90) the FILE-level `state %k` spelling is a silent empty too (it was a whole-file refusal before #314 F-F).  A SILENT WRONG: a member of the next parser-slot batch (s539b's brief gets it at launch).


=== DONE (s539b) ===
Both halves, one reading.  (1) The binder: an interpolation's code span with no `$` / `@` in it was dropped before the fragment read (Binder::_interp_refs / _code_refs), so a bare `%h` inside `@{[ ]}` / `${\ }` was no use; the filters take `%` now, and the code spans themselves are ONE helper, Pl::InterpScan::code_spans (a braced expression's inside, each subscript's guts, a postfix deref's guts), read by the binder and by (2) the rename fixer's hash arm, Parser2::_rename_hash_in_code: inside those spans the code is read as a fragment (Pl::Parser::fragment_doc, as the binder reads it) and each `%bare` Symbol is renamed -- never `%h` in plain text ("100%h").  The three reproducers print perl's output (`k v|1`, `a 1|1|1`, `a 1|1`; the file-level `state %fk` too).  Guards Pl/t/rename-binder-01.t rows 1-4 (fail on c1fe4930) + row 5 (the text control).  Population effect: none beyond the guards (the D0-2 A/B over 3,311 files differed only in t/comp/parser.t, #4921's).  Order matters and the commits show it: the binder half alone makes the rename refuse (rule 12, "cannot reach its use"), the fixer half makes it reach.
